<section xml:id="sec-distance-and-speed" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Distance and Speed</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.0 Measurements and Geometry
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p component="core">
                        2.7 Navigation
                    </p>
                    <p component="essential">
                        2.3 Navigation
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Determine speed in knots and in kilometres per hour
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        12
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- Planning notes (from the Combined Mathematics tab of the Grade 12 Book Structure Mapping sheet)
         Sheet title: Distance and Speed (km/h and knots)
         Content summary: Speed in knots and km/h from distance and time travelled.
         Suggested activity: Learners calculate the speed in knots of a ship sailing between two ports given the time taken. -->

    <!-- Section notes (2026-11)
         SLOs: shared (unmarked in the skeleton; Core (e), Essential (e)). One lesson, flat anatomy.
         Activity: as planning notes (a ship's speed in knots between two ports: Mombasa to Dar es Salaam)
         JSXGraph: none named in the planning notes
         STACK: assets/stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/13-navigation/03-distance-and-speed/ -->

    <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
        <ol>
            <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> a knot is a speed of one nautical mile per hour; speed is distance divided by time; <m>1</m> knot is <m>1.853\,\text{km/h}</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> find speeds, times and new positions for journeys along meridians, along the equator and along parallels; convert between knots and kilometres per hour.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> ferries, cargo ships and flights in East Africa and across the Indian Ocean.</p></li>
        </ol>
    </p>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
            <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners find a ship's speed in knots and in kilometres per hour from the positions of two ports and the times of leaving and arriving.</p></li>
            <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Before part (b), remind learners that minutes are sixtieths of an hour: <m>13\,\text{h}\ 45\,\text{min} = 13.75\,\text{h}</m>, not <m>13.45\,\text{h}</m>. Introduce the word <q>knot</q> only in part (d).</p></li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="act-ship-to-dar-es-salaam">
        <title>The Ship to Dar es Salaam</title>
        <statement>
            <p>A passenger ship sails due south from Mombasa to Dar es Salaam. Take both ports to be on the meridian <m>39^\circ 30'\,\text{E}</m>, with Mombasa at latitude <m>4^\circ 03'\,\text{S}</m> and Dar es Salaam at latitude <m>6^\circ 48'\,\text{S}</m>. The ship leaves Mombasa at 18:00 on Monday and arrives in Dar es Salaam at 07:45 on Tuesday.</p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>How far does the ship sail, in nautical miles?</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Both latitudes are south, so subtract: <m>6^\circ 48' - 4^\circ 03' = 2^\circ 45' = 165'</m>. Along a meridian <m>1'</m> is <m>1</m> nautical mile, so the ship sails <m>165\,\text{nm}</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>How long does the voyage take? Give your answer in hours as a decimal.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>From 18:00 on Monday to midnight is <m>6\,\text{h}</m>; midnight to 07:45 is <m>7\,\text{h}\ 45\,\text{min}</m>. Total <m>13\,\text{h}\ 45\,\text{min} = 13\tfrac{45}{60} = 13.75\,\text{h}</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Find the ship's average speed in nautical miles per hour.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>165 \div 13.75 = 12</m> nautical miles per hour.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Sailors call a speed of <m>1</m> nautical mile per hour a <em>knot</em>. One nautical mile is about <m>1.853\,\text{km}</m>. Find the ship's speed in kilometres per hour.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>12 \times 1.853 = 22.2\,\text{km/h}</m> (to 1 decimal place). The ship's speed is <m>12\,\text{knots}</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>To formalise the knot, the speed formula and the conversions learners used in the activity.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> part (c) gave the speed in nautical miles per hour: that is the speed in knots.</p></li>
                <li><p>Change hours and minutes to hours by dividing the minutes by <m>60</m>. Change a decimal part of an hour back to minutes by multiplying by <m>60</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>Knots to kilometres per hour: multiply by <m>1.853</m>. Kilometres per hour to knots: divide by <m>1.853</m>.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <insight xml:id="insight-speed-in-knots">
        <title>Speed in Knots</title>
        <p>A <term>knot</term> is a speed of one nautical mile per hour. Ships and aircraft give their speeds in knots because their charts measure distance in nautical miles.</p>
        <p>
            <md>
                <mrow>\text{speed} \amp = \frac{\text{distance}}{\text{time}}, \amp \text{time} \amp = \frac{\text{distance}}{\text{speed}}, \amp \text{distance} \amp = \text{speed} \times \text{time}</mrow>
            </md>
            with the distance in nautical miles, the time in hours and the speed in knots.
        </p>
        <p>Since <m>1\,\text{nm} = 1.853\,\text{km}</m>, a speed of <m>1</m> knot is <m>1.853\,\text{km/h}</m>. Write a time in hours as a decimal before dividing: <m>3\,\text{h}\ 20\,\text{min} = 3\tfrac{20}{60}\,\text{h} = 3.333\ldots\,\text{h}</m>.</p>
    </insight>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example xml:id="example-flight-along-the-equator">
        <title>A Flight Along the Equator</title>
        <statement>
            <p>An aircraft flies due west along the equator from longitude <m>37^\circ\,\text{E}</m> to longitude <m>9^\circ\,\text{E}</m>. The flight takes <m>3\,\text{h}\ 30\,\text{min}</m>. Find its speed in knots and in kilometres per hour.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Both longitudes are east, so the difference in longitude is <m>37^\circ - 9^\circ = 28^\circ</m>. The equator is a great circle, so the distance is <m>28 \times 60 = 1680\,\text{nm}</m>. The time is <m>3.5\,\text{h}</m>.
                <md>\text{speed} = \frac{1680}{3.5} = 480\,\text{knots} = 480 \times 1.853 = 889.4\,\text{km/h}</md></p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-sailing-east-along-a-parallel">
        <title>Sailing East Along a Parallel</title>
        <statement>
            <p>A cargo ship leaves Mombasa, taken to be at <m>(4^\circ\,\text{S},\ 39^\circ 40'\,\text{E})</m>, and sails due east along the parallel <m>4^\circ\,\text{S}</m> at <m>15\,\text{knots}</m> for <m>30</m> hours. Find its new position, to the nearest minute.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p><alert>Step 1: distance.</alert> <m>15 \times 30 = 450\,\text{nm}</m>.</p>
            <p><alert>Step 2: change in longitude.</alert> Along the parallel <m>4^\circ\,\text{S}</m>, one degree of longitude is <m>60 \cos 4^\circ\,\text{nm}</m> long. So
                <md>\text{change in longitude} = \frac{450}{60 \cos 4^\circ} = 7.518^\circ = 7^\circ 31'</md>
                to the nearest minute (<m>0.518 \times 60 = 31.1</m>).</p>
            <p><alert>Step 3: new longitude.</alert> Sailing east increases an east longitude: <m>39^\circ 40' + 7^\circ 31' = 47^\circ 11'\,\text{E}</m>. The new position is <m>(4^\circ\,\text{S},\ 47^\circ 11'\,\text{E})</m>.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-from-kmh-to-knots">
        <title>From Kilometres per Hour to Knots</title>
        <statement>
            <p>An airliner cruises at <m>850\,\text{km/h}</m>. Find its speed in knots, and the time it takes to fly <m>1500\,\text{nm}</m>, to the nearest minute.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p><m>850 \div 1.853 = 458.7\,\text{knots}</m>. Time <m>= 1500 \div 458.7 = 3.27\,\text{h}</m>. The decimal part is <m>0.27 \times 60 = 16</m> minutes, so the flight takes <m>3\,\text{h}\ 16\,\text{min}</m>.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

    <p>Type decimals with a point, for example <c>36.8</c>. Type angles in degrees as numbers, without the degree sign. Choose N, S, E or W from each list.</p>
    <p>Give times on the 24-hour clock: type the hours and the minutes in separate boxes, for example 14 and 05 for 14:05.</p>

