<exercise xml:id="ex-new-longitude-after-sailing-along-a-parallel">
    <!--<title></title>-->
    <stack label="stk-new-longitude-after-sailing-along-a-parallel" source="stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/13-navigation/03-distance-and-speed/New-longitude-after-sailing-along-a-parallel.xml"/>
</exercise>

    <exercises xml:id="exercises-distance-and-speed">
        <exercise xml:id="ex-ship-along-meridian-50e">
            <statement><p>A ship sails along the meridian <m>50^\circ\,\text{E}</m> from latitude <m>10^\circ\,\text{S}</m> to latitude <m>2^\circ\,\text{N}</m> in <m>2</m> days <m>12</m> hours. Find its speed in knots and in kilometres per hour.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>12\,\text{knots}</m>; <m>22.2\,\text{km/h}</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>Opposite sides of the equator, so add: <m>10^\circ + 2^\circ = 12^\circ = 720'</m>, so <m>720\,\text{nm}</m>. Time <m>2 \times 24 + 12 = 60\,\text{h}</m>. Speed <m>720 \div 60 = 12\,\text{knots}</m> <m>= 12 \times 1.853 = 22.2\,\text{km/h}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-knots-to-kmh">
            <statement><p>Convert <m>520\,\text{knots}</m> to kilometres per hour.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>963.6\,\text{km/h}</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>One knot is <m>1\,\text{nm}</m> an hour, and <m>1\,\text{nm} = 1.853\,\text{km}</m>. So <m>520 \times 1.853 = 963.56</m>, which is <m>963.6\,\text{km/h}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-flight-along-60n">
            <statement><p>A plane flies along the parallel <m>60^\circ\,\text{N}</m> from longitude <m>10^\circ\,\text{E}</m> to longitude <m>40^\circ\,\text{E}</m> at <m>300\,\text{knots}</m>. How long does the flight take?</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>3</m> hours</p></answer>
            <solution><p>Difference in longitude <m>30^\circ = 1800'</m>. Distance <m>1800 \cos 60^\circ = 900\,\text{nm}</m>. Time <m>900 \div 300 = 3\,\text{h}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-speed-along-20s">
            <statement><p>A ship sails along the parallel <m>20^\circ\,\text{S}</m> from longitude <m>30^\circ\,\text{E}</m> to longitude <m>50^\circ\,\text{E}</m> in <m>48</m> hours. Find its speed in knots and in kilometres per hour, to 1 decimal place.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>23.5\,\text{knots}</m>; <m>43.5\,\text{km/h}</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>Difference in longitude <m>20^\circ = 1200'</m>. Distance <m>1200 \cos 20^\circ = 1127.6\,\text{nm}</m>. Speed <m>1127.6 \div 48 = 23.5\,\text{knots}</m>; <m>23.49 \times 1.853 = 43.5\,\text{km/h}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-new-position-25s">
            <title>Challenge: A New Position</title>
            <statement><p>A ship at <m>(25^\circ\,\text{S},\ 80^\circ\,\text{E})</m> sails due west along its parallel at <m>18\,\text{knots}</m> for <m>40</m> hours. Find its new position, to the nearest minute.</p></statement>
            <hint><p>Find the distance first, then divide by the length of one degree of longitude on this parallel.</p></hint>
            <answer><p><m>(25^\circ\,\text{S},\ 66^\circ 46'\,\text{E})</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>Distance <m>18 \times 40 = 720\,\text{nm}</m>. Change in longitude <m>720 \div (60 \cos 25^\circ) = 13.241^\circ = 13^\circ 14'</m>. Sailing west decreases an east longitude: <m>80^\circ - 13^\circ 14' = 66^\circ 46'\,\text{E}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <table xml:id="table-differentiation-distance-and-speed">
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Work through the activity and <xref ref="example-flight-along-the-equator"/>. Give a card: <q>h min to hours: divide the minutes by 60</q>. Assign <xref ref="ex-ship-along-meridian-50e"/> and <xref ref="ex-knots-to-kmh"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-ship-along-meridian-50e"/> to <xref ref="ex-new-position-25s"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-new-position-25s"/>. Extension: two ships leave the same point on the parallel <m>25^\circ\,\text{S}</m>, one due east at <m>15\,\text{knots}</m> and one due west at <m>15\,\text{knots}</m>. After <m>24</m> hours, how far apart are they along the parallel, and what is their difference in longitude? (Answer: <m>720\,\text{nm}</m>; <m>720 \div (60 \cos 25^\circ) = 13.24^\circ = 13^\circ 14'</m>.)</cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>

    <p component="lesson-plan">Closing question for the class: where in Kenya is speed measured in knots? (Ships at Mombasa and Lamu, aircraft at Jomo Kenyatta and Wilson airports.)</p>

